{2} = \\frac{r(r-1)(r-2)(r-3)(r-4)}{120} \\frac{b(b-1)}{2}$\n\nEquating LHS and RHS:\n$\\frac{r(r-1)(r-2)(r-3)}{4!} \\cdot \\frac{b(b-1)(b-2)}{3!} = \\frac{r(r-1)(r-2)(r-3)(r-4)}{5!} \\cdot \\frac{b(b-1)}{2!}$\n\nCancel common terms from the numerators. We know $r \\ge 7$ and $b \\ge 7$.\n$r(r-1)(r-2)(r-3) \\neq 0$ and $b(b-1)(b-2) \\neq 0$.\nActually, wait. If $r < 4$, $\\binom{r}{4}=0$, etc. But the problem states $r, b \\ge 7$. So the counts are valid.\nDivide both sides by $\\binom{r}{4} \\binom{b}{2}$ or something simpler?\nLet's rewrite the equation:\n$\\binom{r}{4} \\binom{b}{3} = \\binom{r}{5} \\binom{b}{2}$\nThis is equivalent to choosing 4 Red and 3 Blue having the same probability as choosing 5 Red and 2 Blue. This is a bit unusual. Usually problems involve specific probabilities like \"at least one\". Here it's a specific count condition.\nLet's write the combinations explicitly:\n$\\frac{r!}{4!(r-4)!} \\frac{b!}{3!(b-3)!} = \\frac{r!}{5!(r-5)!} \\frac{b!}{2!(b-2)!}$\n\nThis equation must hold. Let's cancel the factorials where possible.\n$\\frac{r(r-1)(r-2)(r-3)}{4!} \\cdot \\frac{b(b-1)(b-2)}{3!} = \\frac{r(r-1)(r-2)(r-3)(r-4)}{5!} \\cdot \\frac{b(b-1)}{2!}$\nCancel $r(r-1)(r-2)(r-3)$ from both sides (since $r \\ge 7$, these are positive integers).\nAlso note $b \\ge 7$, so $b(b-1) > 0$.\nSo, $\\frac{1}{4!} b(b-1)(b-2) = \\frac{r-4}{5!}$. Wait, I missed a factor on the right side.\nLet's write it out again.\n$\\binom{r}{4} \\binom{b}{3} = \\binom{r}{5} \\binom{b}{2}$\n$\\frac{r!}{4!(r-4)!} \\cdot \\frac{b!}{3!(b-3)!} = \\frac{r!}{5!(r-5)!} \\cdot \\frac{b!}{2!(b-2)!}$\n\nLet's divide by $n(n-1)...$ no, that's getting messy.\nLet's just use the property of binomial coefficients.\n$\\binom{r}{4} \\binom{b}{3} = \\binom{r}{5} \\binom{b}{2}$\n$\\frac{b!}{(b-3)!} \\cdot \\frac{r!}{4!(r-4)!} ...$ wait.\nLet's rearrange the equation:\n$\\frac{\\binom{r}{4}}{\\binom{r}{5}} = \\frac{\\binom{b}{2}}{\\binom{b}{3}}$\nUsing the definition of combinations $\\binom{n}{k} = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}$.\nLHS ratio: $\\frac{r!}{4!(r-4)!} / \\frac{r!}{5!(r-5)!} = \\frac{5!(r-5)!}{4!(r-4)!} = \\frac{5}{r-4}$.\nWait, this is if I move $\\binom{r}{5}$ to the other side.\nLet's rewrite the equality:\n$\\binom{r}{4} \\binom{b}{3} = \\binom{r}{5} \\binom{b}{2}$\n$\\frac{r!}{4!(r-4)!} \\cdot \\frac{b!}{3!(b-3)!} = \\frac{r!}{5!(r-5)!} \\cdot \\frac{b!}{2!(b-2)!}$\nDivide both sides by $\\frac{r!}{(r-5)!} \\frac{b!}{(b-3)!}$ which isn't quite right.\nLet's simplify term by term.\n$\\frac{\\binom{r}{4}}{\\binom{r}{5}} = \\frac{\\binom{b}{2}}{\\binom{b}{3}}$\nWe know $\\binom{n}{k} = n!/k!(n-i)!$. Or $\\binom{n}{k} = \\frac{n}{k-w} \\binom{n-1}{w-1} ...$ No.\nRecall $\\binom{n}{k} / \\binom{n}{j}$.\n$\\frac{\\binom{r}{4}}{\\binom{r}{5}} = \\frac{r!/4!(r-4)!}{r!/5!(r-5)!} = \\frac{5}{r-4}$.\nSimilarly, $\\frac{\\binom{b}{2}}{\\binom{b}{3}} = \\frac{b!/2!(b-2)!}{b!/3!(b-3)!} = \\frac{3}{b-2} \\times \\frac{(r-5)!}{(r-4)!} ...$ wait.\nLet's write it as:\n$\\frac{r(r-1)(r-2)(r-3)}{24} \\cdot \\frac{b(b-1)(b-2)}{6} = \\frac{r(r-1)(r-2)(r-3)(r-4)}{120} \\cdot \\frac{b(b-1)}{2}$\nLet's simplify.\n$\\frac{1}{4 \\cdot 3} \\cdot (r)_4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3!} (b)_3 = \\frac{1}{5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1} (r)_5 \\frac{1}{2!} (b)_2$ where $(n)_k is falling factorial.\nActually, $\\binom{r}{4} = \\frac{r}{4} \\binom{r-1}{3}$. Or better, just substitute the expression for the combination directly.\n$\\binom{r}{4} = \\frac{r!}{4! (r-4)!}$.\n$\\binom{b}{3} = \\frac{b!}{3! (b-3)!}$.\nRHS: $\\binom{r}{5} = \\frac{r(r-1)(r-2)(r-3)(r-4)}{120}$. $\\binom{b}{2} = \\frac{b(b-1)}{2}$.\n\nSo the equation is:\n$\\binom{r}{4} \\binom{b}{3} = \\binom{r}{5} \\binom{b}{2}$.\n\nLet's cancel out the parts that are common to both sides:\n$\\binom{r}{4} / \\binom{r}{5} = \\binom{b}{2} / \\binom{b}{3}$.\nNo, that was not correct.\nLHS: $\\binom{r}{4} \\binom{b}{3}$. RHS: $\\binom{r}{5} \\binom{b}{2}$.\nThe common terms are $\\binom{r}{4}$ vs $\\binom{r}{5}$. No The terms wit